      ["Open the surgical incision, evacuate purulent collections, debride non-viable tissue, and pack with moist sterile gauze", "The patient presents on postoperative day 7 with a surgical site infection (SSI, wound infection). Postoperative fever chronology follows the classical '5 Ws': Day 1–2: Wind (atelectasis); Day 3–5: Water (urinary tract infection); Day 4–6: Walking (deep venous thrombosis / pulmonary embolism); Day 5–8: Wound (surgical site infection); and > Day 7–10: Wonder drugs (drug fever) or deep intra-abdominal abscess. For an active superficial/deep surgical site infection with wound erythema, induration, and purulent drainage, the PRIMARY AND MANDATORY FIRST-LINE TREATMENT IS MECHANICAL: removal of sutures or staples, opening the wound, evacuating infected purulent collections, debriding any necrotic tissue, and placing moist gauze packings for secondary intention healing. Antibiotics alone cannot sterilize an enclosed purulent fluid collection, and are added only if there is significant surrounding cellulitis (> 5 cm) or systemic sepsis."],
