      ["Nasogastric tube decompression, nil per os (NPO), intravenous fluid resuscitation, and serial abdominal examinations", "The patient has an uncomplicated, adhesive mechanical small bowel obstruction (SBO). Intra-abdominal adhesions from prior surgeries are the leading cause of SBO (> 60–75% of cases). In the absence of signs of bowel strangulation, ischemia, or necrosis (which are signaled by fever, tachycardia, localized peritonitis, leukocytosis, metabolic acidosis, or lack of bowel wall enhancement on CT), INITIAL MANAGEMENT OF ADHESIVE SBO IS NON-OPERATIVE (CONSERVATIVE): (1) Nasogastric (NG) tube placement to low intermittent suction to decompress the proximal gastrointestinal tract, relieve nausea/distension, and prevent pulmonary aspiration; (2) Nil per os (NPO); (3) Intravenous fluid replacement to correct volume contraction; and (4) Close clinical surveillance with serial abdominal examinations every 4 to 6 hours. Approximately 70% to 80% of adhesive SBOs resolve spontaneously with conservative management within 48 to 72 hours."],
