      ["Perform an urgent upper gastrointestinal CT scan with water-soluble oral and IV contrast or prompt surgical exploration", "The patient is exhibiting the classic and often solitary early sign of an ANASTOMOTIC LEAK following Roux-en-Y gastric bypass: UNEXPLAINED PERSISTENT TACHYCARDIA (heart rate > 120/min). Anastomotic leak (most commonly at the gastrojejunal anastomosis) is the leading cause of mortality following bariatric surgery. In severely obese patients, the classic physical signs of peritonitis (guarding, rigidity) are frequently masked by a thick abdominal wall and altered anatomy; fever may be absent initially. Left shoulder pain (referred diaphragmatic irritation, Kehr sign) and oliguria further support a leak. Immediate investigation with an URGENT CT SCAN OF THE ABDOMEN/PELVIS WITH WATER-SOLUBLE ORAL AND IV CONTRAST (or upper GI fluoroscopic swallow) is mandatory. If imaging is equivocal or the patient deteriorates, immediate surgical re-exploration (laparoscopy or laparotomy) is life-saving."],
