      ["Immobilize the wrist in a thumb spica splint and arrange repeat plain radiographs in 10 to 14 days", "The patient has clinical suspicion for an occult scaphoid (carpal navicular) fracture following a fall on an outstretched hand (FOOSH), confirmed by exquisite point tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox, pain with axial thumb compression, and scaphoid tubercle tenderness. Initial plain radiographs have a false-negative rate of up to 20% to 30% for acute, non-displaced scaphoid fractures because hairline fractures are not visible until osteoclastic bone resorption widens the fracture line over 7 to 14 days. CRITICAL VASCULAR PRINCIPLE: The scaphoid receives its blood supply via retrograde branches of the radial artery entering at the distal pole; fractures across the scaphoid waist or proximal pole disrupt blood flow, predisposing to AVASCULAR NECROSIS (osteonecrosis) and nonunion if unrecognized and un-immobilized. Therefore, any patient with snuffbox tenderness following trauma MUST BE TREATED AS A FRACTURE REGARDLESS OF NORMAL INITIAL X-RAYS: apply a THUMB SPICA SPLINT (or cast) and repeat radiographs in 10 to 14 days (or obtain an immediate wrist MRI)."],
